WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.000
Thor, the Norse god of thunder, has gone through&nbsp;
many changes during his time in the Marvel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.320
Cinematic Universe, including his personality.&nbsp;
Alongside his learned wisdom and merit, Thor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:19.120
has become quite the jokester. Here are the god of&nbsp;
thunder's jokes in the MCU, ranked worst to best.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:25.840
The first entry into our rundown of the god of&nbsp;
thunder's jokes in the MCU comes in his very first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:32.400
appearance, 2011's eponymous solo outing. Having&nbsp;
just been put in place by his father Odin, played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:37.440
by Anthony Hopkins, Thor's trying to convince&nbsp;
his compatriots to go with him to Jotunheim.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:42.160
He hopes they’ll see how the Frost Giants&nbsp;
managed to break through Asgard's defenses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.760
in their attempt to steal the&nbsp;
casket. As Lady Sif reminds him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:52.000
Odin forbade such an action, but Thor&nbsp;
persists in his manipulation. Thor asks,

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.400
"My friends, have you forgotten&nbsp;
all that we have done together?"

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.880
For Fandral and Hogun, Thor points out that he'd&nbsp;
led them into the most glorious of battles. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:05.200
sway Volstagg, he reminds him that the&nbsp;
delicacies he thought were so succulent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:10.720
that he felt he'd died and gone to Valhalla.&nbsp;
And then, to Sif, he posed a simple question.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.760
"And who proved wrong all who&nbsp;
scoffed at the idea that a young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.960
maiden could be one of the fiercest&nbsp;
warriors this realm has ever known?”

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:17.460
“I did.”

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:19.840
“True, but I supported you."

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:23.200
It's a coin flip as to whether you&nbsp;
think he was trying to steal Sif's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:27.680
thunder — pun obviously intended — but&nbsp;
he did give her due credit in the end.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.440
In Thor's second standalone movie, "The Dark&nbsp;
World," he's cracking more jokes than in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:38.640
first, probably as a result of hanging out with&nbsp;
Tony Stark in 2012's first "Avengers" film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.680
When Lady Sif is leading Asgard's armies and&nbsp;
the Warriors Three in battle on Hogun's home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:48.800
world of Vanaheim and clearly thinks things are&nbsp;
going swimmingly, Thor shows up on the scene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.360
He quickly takes out some enemy combatants&nbsp;
with his trusty hammer, Mjolnir, and Sif is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:58.560
perturbed at his arrival. She complains,&nbsp;
and Thor responds with a well-timed joke.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.640
"I've got this completely under control.”

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:02.160
“Is that why everything's on fire?"

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:04.880
Total burn, Thor!

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:12.080
As the battle on Vanaheim rages on in "Thor 2,"&nbsp;
with the enemy surrounding Asgard's warriors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:15.680
an angry roar stops the fight in mid action.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:21.520
Everyone looks around for the source of such&nbsp;
a ferocious rumble. That's when a giant Kronan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:27.920
warrior appears and marches toward Thor. MCU&nbsp;
fans who've seen "Thor: Ragnarok may think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:33.440
this massive humanoid warrior made of stone is&nbsp;
the same type of being as Korg, played by Taika&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:38.880
Waititi. They'd be right in thinking this is a&nbsp;
Kronan and thus the same species as Korg. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:44.800
according to the DVD commentary for "Thor:&nbsp;
The Dark World" this Kronan actually is Korg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:49.840
and the version who appears in "Ragnarok" has been&nbsp;
retconned by Waititi, who also directed the film.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:56.960
Now, Thor's a big dude, but this version of Korg&nbsp;
is massive and the Norse god of thunder is about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:03.840
waist height on the brute. But&nbsp;
Thor, of course, isn't fazed.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.040
"I accept your surrender."

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:12.800
And when Thor backs up his joke by smashing&nbsp;
Korg to pieces, he gets in another zinger.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:13.360
"Anyone else?"

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:19.600
When Rhodey, played by Don Cheadle, took the Iron&nbsp;
Man Mark II armor during "Iron Man 2" and became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:25.520
War Machine, he stepped into the big leagues.&nbsp;
Viewers catch a conversation in Avengers: Age of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:31.520
Ultron where Rhodey is regaling Tony Stark, Maria&nbsp;
Hill, and Thor with a story of his own adventures.

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:35.120
"So I take the tank, fly it&nbsp;
right up to the general's palace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:38.160
drop it at his feet. I'm like,&nbsp;
'Boom, you looking for this?'"

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:44.320
A confused Thor asks if that's the whole story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:49.920
Rhodey says that it is, that it’s a War Machine&nbsp;
story. Thor laughs and responds, saying,

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:52.880
"Oh, it's very good then. It's impressive."

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.240
Quality save, Thor. Fortunately for Rhodey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:00.880
the story does indeed kill later on when&nbsp;
he tells it to some non-Avenger partygoers.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:07.200
As Thor attempts to smuggle Loki out of Asgard in&nbsp;
"The Dark World," Loki is doing one of the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:12.800
he does best: annoying Thor. Loki first&nbsp;
takes the form of an Asgardian warrior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:17.440
then takes up his own likeness again and&nbsp;
makes his brother look like Sif. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:22.240
Loki takes the form of one of Thor's more recent&nbsp;
allies, Captain America, played by Chris Evans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:26.560
and offers to have a rousing discussion&nbsp;
about truth, honor, and patriotism.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:29.600
Loki realizes that nagging Thor isn't working out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:33.360
so he finally just asks Thor for a&nbsp;
weapon to make the escape easier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:38.640
Thor locks eyes with Loki and appears to be&nbsp;
rustling around for something for Loki to wield.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.840
"At last, a little common sense."

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:46.320
As it turns out, Thor did use his common&nbsp;
sense: he handcuffed the trickster.

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:48.080
"I thought you liked tricks."

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:53.600
It's no secret Loki has done a great many&nbsp;
horrible things. He's betrayed pretty much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:58.960
everyone he knows, Thor included. He's&nbsp;
manipulated everyone he's ever met,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:05.440
Thor included. He's tried to kill pretty much&nbsp;
everyone with whom he's come into contact, Thor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:10.800
included. These shenanigans play out over the&nbsp;
course of the first two Thor solo films and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:15.840
first Avengers movie. When we get to "Ragnarok,"&nbsp;
it's more of the same, whether on Asgard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:21.600
Earth, or Sakaar. As Thor, Valkyrie, and Bruce&nbsp;
Banner, discuss their exit strategy and how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:26.720
get through a wormhole terrifyingly dubbed&nbsp;
the Devil's Anus, our favorite trickster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:31.680
chimes in with an offer of assistance, causing&nbsp;
Banner to pull the other two into a sidebar.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:36.880
Banner says that he had just been talking to Loki,&nbsp;
and he was perfectly ready to kill any of them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:42.000
Valkyrie agrees, mentioning Loki had tried to kill&nbsp;
her. Thor, who is the obvious expert on Loki's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:46.800
attempts at murder, concurs, noting his adopted&nbsp;
brother has tried to take his life on, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:50.560
"many, many occasions" before&nbsp;
describing one in particular.

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:52.160
"He transformed himself into a snake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:55.360
and he knows that I love snakes. So I&nbsp;
went to pick up the snake to admire it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:58.880
and he transformed back into himself, and he&nbsp;
was like 'Ya! It's me!' and he stabbed me."

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.200
It’s no secret that Loki has a thing for&nbsp;
stabbing people — especially in the back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:05.840
but at least Thor got a funny story out of it.

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:10.800
Dr. Bruce Banner deals with a lot of doubts&nbsp;
and insecurities — given the great beast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:15.520
that lives within him. While he's able to summon&nbsp;
the Hulk at will, getting the big guy to stand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:21.200
down is an entirely different matter. But, by the&nbsp;
time "Age of Ultron" rolls around, he seems to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:27.200
have developed a system of deescalation with Black&nbsp;
Widow, involving holding hands and a lullaby. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:31.520
doesn't stop him from being dejected about&nbsp;
the entire situation after they had to call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:36.160
a Code Green in their attack on Strucker's&nbsp;
compound in the opening sequence of the film.

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:39.440
As they're flying back to&nbsp;
Avengers HQ aboard the Quinjet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:44.480
Natasha asks Bruce when he's going to trust&nbsp;
her and he responds with the obvious answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:50.400
that it's not her that he doesn't trust. So she&nbsp;
enlists another teammate — perhaps the wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:56.480
one — to weigh in, asking Thor what he thinks of&nbsp;
the Hulk. Thor responds with good humor, as usual.

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:59.520
"The gates of Hell are filled&nbsp;
with the screams of his victims."

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:03.760
When he sees that neither Natasha or&nbsp;
Banner is in the mood for his jokes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:05.600
he quickly walks back his remarks.

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:08.480
"No, no, wounded screams. Mainly whimpering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:13.760
a great deal of complaining and&nbsp;
tales of sprained deltoids and gout."

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:16.240
Another quality save.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:22.960
In "Ragnarok," Thor proves his mettle by surviving&nbsp;
an encounter with the Grandmaster's champion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:28.160
aka The Hulk. Afterwards, Thor's living&nbsp;
conditions improve immensely. He's no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:33.120
longer living among the rabble of lower&nbsp;
card fighters like Korg. As a main eventer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:37.360
he's in penthouse-level quarters with&nbsp;
his opponent, the Hulk. There's training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:43.440
equipment befitting a champion, including&nbsp;
what every noble warrior needs: a hot tub.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:48.800
Great for soaking sore muscles, it's an&nbsp;
essential tool for a fighter's rest and recovery.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:53.680
We're guessing most athletes choose to soak in&nbsp;
a pair of trunks or other undergarment when in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:58.640
a group setting, but not our Hulk, which Thor&nbsp;
finds out when pressing him for information.

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:03.360
Thor asks him how he arrived on Sakaar. Hulk&nbsp;
explains that he came there when the Quinjet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:09.120
he took at the end of "Age of Ultron" crash landed&nbsp;
there. Well, he offered the one-word response of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:14.560
"Quinjet" and then mimed a crash with his hand,&nbsp;
but it's all there in the subtext. Naturally Thor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:18.960
wants to know where the Quinjet is now so he&nbsp;
could potentially use it himself. Having just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:24.800
demonstrated his propensity for showing rather&nbsp;
than telling, Hulk gets out of the tub fully nude,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:30.640
to point out where the crashed Quinjet is,&nbsp;
giving Thor an eyeful as he walks to the window.

00:08:31.101 --> 00:08:38.240
"That's, ah, naked. Very naked.&nbsp;
Hmmm. That's in my brain now."

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:44.480
Thor's only got a few friends at his disposal when&nbsp;
stranded on Sakaar in "Ragnarok" and they're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:50.720
exactly keen on helping him escape. Valkyrie can't&nbsp;
seem to stop drinking or using that obedience disk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:55.520
on him, so she's not going to be of much help.&nbsp;
Korg and Miek are willing but they're locked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.520 --> 00:09:01.360
up with all the other slaves — sorry, prisoners&nbsp;
with jobs — so he can't expect much out of them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:07.200
He needs Hulk's help and he’s going to do his best&nbsp;
to convince the great green brute to help him out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:11.200
even if it means a bit of&nbsp;
manipulation. Thor tells Hulk,

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:14.000
"This is what friends do,&nbsp;
they support each other."

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:18.720
Even so, Hulk insists that Thor&nbsp;
is Banner's friend and not his.

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:19.520
"Banner's friend.”

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:23.040
“I don't even like Banner! He’s all, 'I'm&nbsp;
into numbers and science and stuff.'"

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:28.320
Once Hulk reverts to Bruce Banner, having&nbsp;
seen Natasha's message aboard the Quinjet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:32.720
he just wrecked, Thor tries a bit of&nbsp;
the same with Hulk's human counterpart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:36.800
Though, it may be with perhaps a bit less finesse.

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:41.920
"I just told you if I turn into the Hulk , I'm&nbsp;
never gonna come back again and you don't care!"

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:45.920
Thor has upset Banner and has made him feel used,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:50.720
especially when Thor says that Hulk is the&nbsp;
fire that he needs to defeat his sister.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:56.640
Banner tells Thor that he’s not his friend. Thor,&nbsp;
then, reverses what he said to Hulk earlier:

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:02.400
"No, I don’t even like the Hulk. He's like&nbsp;
'Roar, smash, smash, smash.' I prefer you."

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:08.960
As "Endgame" is coming to a close, it ties up&nbsp;
its myriad loose ends in an extended finale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:12.480
We see Thor entrust the future of&nbsp;
the New Asgard colony on Earth to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:15.520
Valkyrie before boarding the ship&nbsp;
with the Guardians of the Galaxy.

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:21.520
"The Asgardians of the Galaxy, back&nbsp;
together again. Where to first?"

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:26.960
On board, Thor immediately makes himself at&nbsp;
home, and changes the map that Star-Lord,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:33.120
aka Peter Quill is looking at. Star-Lord, annoyed,&nbsp;
changes it back and decides it's time to lay down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:37.680
the law with the newbie. After a brief back&nbsp;
and forth about who's in charge on the ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:40.240
Drax suggests they fight for the leadership role.

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:42.960
"I got some blasters unless&nbsp;
you guys want to use knives.”

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:46.320
“Oh, yes, please, use knives."

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:50.320
Quill and Thor both laugh, and Thor&nbsp;
cheerfully explains that they don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:52.880
have to fight, because it's clear who's in charge.

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:56.560
"There shall be no knifing one another;&nbsp;
everybody knows who's in charge."

00:10:56.560 --> 00:11:04.160
After a brief, uncertain pause, Quill asks,&nbsp;
quote, "Me, right?" Thor's response says it all.

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:10.720
"Yes, you. Of course, of course, of course."

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:17.360
About 50 minutes into "The Avengers," the team&nbsp;
sits down for its first unofficial team meeting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:22.400
on the bridge of the massive SHIELD helicarrier.&nbsp;
Before Tony Stark shows up, Thor discusses with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:28.240
the other heroes the film's primary antagonist,&nbsp;
Loki. Nick Fury has just gotten done interrogating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:33.040
the god of mischief and Cap asks Thor what&nbsp;
he thinks his brother's plan might be and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:39.040
wonders why Loki let them take him into custody.&nbsp;
After all, Cap points out, Loki can't exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:44.240
lead his army while aboard the helicarrier.&nbsp;
Banner posits that they shouldn't focus on Loki.

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:47.600
"That guy's brain is a bag full of&nbsp;
cats. You could smell crazy on him."

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:53.840
The god of thunder takes a surprising amount&nbsp;
of offense to this. He warns Banner to watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:58.800
his words, saying that while Loki might be&nbsp;
a villain, he is still a prince of Asgard.

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:00.160
"And he is my brother.”

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:01.920
“He killed 80 people in two days.”

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:03.600
“He's adopted."

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:08.800
Things get tense in the elevator scene&nbsp;
in "Ragnarok," as Loki leads Thor to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:13.680
Grandmaster's ship. Loki says he's probably&nbsp;
better off staying on Sakaar and Thor agrees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:17.920
to his surprise. Thor says Sakaar is&nbsp;
perfect for his brother, that it's, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:24.960
“savage, chaotic, lawless.” Thor thinks Loki will&nbsp;
do great there. Loki's taken aback, asking Thor if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:31.280
he truly thinks so little of him and Thor replies&nbsp;
that he actually once thought the world of Loki.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:36.320
But, while there might still be some&nbsp;
good in the cantankerous god of mischief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:41.760
he concedes that've been walking separate&nbsp;
paths for a long time. After a pregnant pause,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:47.680
Loki somberly says it's probably for the best that&nbsp;
they never see each other again, and Thor twists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:52.880
the knife, patting him on the back and reminding&nbsp;
him by saying that it’s what he’s always wanted.

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:59.600
Suddenly, Thor has an idea and brightens the mood,&nbsp;
suggesting they do the tried and true "Get Help"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.600 --> 00:13:04.880
routine. Loki declines. Thor reminds him that&nbsp;
it works every time and Loki says that it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:11.600
humiliating. Thor asks if Loki has a better plan,&nbsp;
which he doesn’t. So, Thor concludes that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:19.109
should do it, while Loki is still insistent that&nbsp;
they will not. Spoiler alert: they do "Get Help."

00:13:19.109 --> 00:13:22.400
"Get help! Please! My brother,&nbsp;
he’s dying! Get help! Help him!"

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:30.000
Afterward, Thor has clearly enjoyed the routine,&nbsp;
while Loki complains that he still hates it.

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:31.440
"It's humiliating.”

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:32.240
“Not for me it's not."

00:13:32.880 --> 00:13:37.280
When it comes to comparing adopted siblings&nbsp;
Thor and Loki, their respective strengths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:41.600
are pretty clear. Thor is the clear winner&nbsp;
when it comes to physical might and combat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:46.640
while Loki is obviously the more cerebral of&nbsp;
the two demigods. It should be no surprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.640 --> 00:13:52.080
considering one is the warrior god of thunder and&nbsp;
the other is the trickster god of mischief. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:57.200
such, it's rare that Thor gets one over one the&nbsp;
sneaky Loki. Even so, Loki does underestimates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.200 --> 00:14:02.960
Thor, having gotten the better of him for so long.&nbsp;
But Thor is capable of growth, as he demonstrates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:08.080
while they're heading to the Grandmaster's ship in&nbsp;
"Ragnarok," and he posits that Loki has stagnated.

00:14:08.080 --> 00:14:13.120
After they perform the hilarious "Get Help" sneak&nbsp;
attack routine, Loki slinks off to a control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:18.560
panel to turn Thor in while a magic double walks&nbsp;
alongside his brother. Of course, Thor knew that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:23.280
would happen too. During his big speech in the&nbsp;
elevator, before suggesting they do "Get Help,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:28.080
Thor planted one of the Grandmaster's obedience&nbsp;
disks on Loki when patting him on the back. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:33.280
triggering the device in the hangar, he explains&nbsp;
that Loki's becoming predictable. Then, he makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:38.480
sure to put the remote for the obedience disk&nbsp;
somewhere Loki can reach it. Okay, not really.

00:14:38.480 --> 00:14:40.560
"I'll just put this over here for you."

00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:44.720
The open scene of “Ragnarok”&nbsp;
begins with Thor narrating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:48.000
supposedly to us, but maybe&nbsp;
to the skeleton in his cage.

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:52.640
“I know what you’re thinking, 'Oh no!&nbsp;
Thor's in a cage, how did this happen?'"

00:14:53.680 --> 00:14:57.840
He explains that, sometimes, you have to get&nbsp;
captured in order to get a candid response&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.840 --> 00:15:02.400
from someone. Before the bottom drops out&nbsp;
of the cage and the god of thunder is left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:06.560
dangling by a length of chain, Thor&nbsp;
wonders aloud how long they'll be there.

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:13.360
But where exactly is he? Thor's location, as&nbsp;
it turns out, is the fiery realm of Muspelheim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.360 --> 00:15:16.960
inhabited by fire demons, the&nbsp;
chief of whom is Thor's captor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:25.840
Surtur. But even chained up in front of a fire&nbsp;
demon, Thor manages to sneak in a good joke.

00:15:25.840 --> 00:15:28.550
"Thor, son of Odin.”

00:15:28.550 --> 00:15:32.320
“Surtur, son of ... a b----!"

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:35.520
Clearly, Thor's been spending&nbsp;
too much time around Tony Stark.

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:44.240
Thor's best joke in the MCU is a quip that's&nbsp;
a bit understated but absolutely hilarious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:48.160
After preventing the end of the world in&nbsp;
"Ragnarok," Thor returns to Asgard from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:52.880
Muspelheim, having ripped Surtur's tiara off.&nbsp;
After witnessing someone he's pretty sure is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.880 --> 00:15:58.480
not Odin taking in a live performance of "The&nbsp;
Tragedy of Loki of Asgard," Thor unmasks Loki&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.480 --> 00:16:03.200
with the threat of splattering his brother with&nbsp;
Mjolnir. Then he demands to see his father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.200 --> 00:16:08.400
whom he believes Loki has spirited away. To&nbsp;
accomplish that, a pre-Executioner Skurge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:13.440
uses the bifrost to send them to Earth, where Loki&nbsp;
and Thor witness the demolition of the Shady Acres&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:19.200
Care Home in New York City. Loki swears he left&nbsp;
Odin right there, to which Thor asks if he means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:24.160
on the sidewalk where they're standing or where&nbsp;
the building is being demolished. Unsurprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:29.600
Loki doesn't think he's to blame. After another&nbsp;
sarcastic remark from Thor, Loki responds,

00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:32.560
“How was I supposed to know? I can't&nbsp;
see into the future; I'm not a witch.”

00:16:32.560 --> 00:16:34.240
“No? Then why do you dress like one?"

00:16:35.280 --> 00:16:40.560
Funny and true. With the black-on-black&nbsp;
suit and shoulder-length greasy hair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:44.240
Loki indeed looks like he just&nbsp;
stepped off the Hogwarts Express.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.240 --> 00:16:50.080
Take your best guess which House he'd belong&nbsp;
to. We're pretty sure it wouldn't be Hufflepuff.

